---
// HOME — rewritten 2026-09-07 for a single audience: the buyer.
//
// Three decisions from the founder drive this file:
//
//   1. THE HERO LEADS WITH THE CAPABILITY, not the problem and not the category. "The first
//      neural network that names its own broken part." NOT "the first" — that was a priority
//      claim, and a priority claim is the one sentence on a site like this that a technical
//      reader attacks before reading anything else. It cannot be defended (somebody will always
//      produce a 2019 paper), it is not needed by the argument, and losing it costs nothing: the
//      sentence is just as strong describing the property instead of claiming precedence.
//      The evidence row underneath is then
//      earning that sentence rather than decorating an abstract one.
//   2. THE SLOT COUNT IS GONE. The scarcity is real and stays — three Charter slots a quarter is
//      a capacity policy — but the previous "2 of 3 remain" meter rendered one slot as already
//      taken, which asserts a customer commitment. Policy, not a countdown.
//   3. WRITTEN FOR THE BUYER. Investors have /invest and it is in the nav; researchers have
//      /paper. The old closing grid put an investor card at the same weight as the commercial
//      one on a page that has ten seconds to qualify an ML lead or a risk owner. It is gone.
//
// SECTION ORDER is a funnel, and the order is the argument:
//   hero (what it is) -> problem (why you care) -> mechanism (why nobody else has it)
//   -> features (what you get) -> evidence (proof) -> markets (is this me?)
//   -> why now (why not next year) -> charter (what to do)
//
// TWO SECTIONS ARE NEW. "What it costs you today" and "Why now" did not exist on this page. The
// first is the agitation step the page never had — it went from a headline straight to a
// mechanism explanation, which asks a visitor to care about a solution before naming a problem.
// The second is the only urgency argument here tied to something external and dated rather than
// to our own release schedule.
//
// REMOVED FROM THIS PAGE: the adversarial-weight-injection limitation paragraph. It is a real
// result and it stays published — it is on the technology page under "What was not tested" and in /faq at
// full length. The second section of the homepage, before the offer has been made, is the wrong
// place for it: it was answering an objection no visitor had formed yet.
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import Eyebrow from '../components/Eyebrow.astro';
import HeroVisual from '../components/HeroVisual.astro';
import SectorGraphic from '../components/SectorGraphic.astro';
import { sectors } from '../data/sectors';
import { features } from '../data/features';
import Button from '../components/Button.astro';

// Which markets each use case serves, read from data/sectors.ts so the mapping cannot drift from
// the sector pages. `12 §3` "Where Each Product Lands" is the source: the Proofreader is the only
// one that enters every market, and saying so is worth more than listing five names.
const marketsFor = (id: string) => {
	const served = sectors.filter((s) => s.features.includes(id));
	if (served.length === sectors.length) return 'Serves all five markets';
	return served.map((s) => s.name).join(' · ');
};


// THE COST TABLE. Rewritten 2026-09-07. Two prior versions of this section were wrong in
// instructive ways, and the notes are here so neither gets rebuilt.
//
// VERSION 1 was three generic cards with no market specificity.
//
// VERSION 2 was a five-market, three-column ledger with four sub-points per market. Two faults:
//
//   LENGTH. Roughly 700 words in the second section of a homepage. It was a document, and the
//   reader it is written for scans this section in about ten seconds.
//
//   THE FIGURES DID NOT HOLD. They were four different kinds of number presented as one kind:
//     · $200M–$500M     what a frontier run costs — not a loss anyone books
//     · $145.5B         top-20 pharma R&D: a TAM figure, and `12 §2` is explicit that TAM is
//                       the wrong lens and the right question is what the failure already costs
//     · 47.3% / 10.0%   our own parameter-matched baselines, presented as the industry's loss.
//                       Circular, and a technical reader catches it immediately.
//   Only the retrain-from-scratch figure was a cost an actual buyer actually pays.
//
// SO: one row per market, one consequence, one cost — and the cost column says what is true
// rather than forcing a currency figure into markets that have no published one. Three markets
// carry a number because a number exists. Two carry the consequence, because inventing a figure
// there would be the only fabricated thing on the page.
const ledger = [
	{
		id: 'ai-training',
		market: 'AI Training',
		breaks: 'A run degrades silently. The loss curve moves only once the damage is thousands of steps old, so recovery means discarding everything since the last checkpoint.',
		cost: '$16,000–$24,000',
		costUnit: 'per two-hour recovery',
	},
	{
		id: 'medical-ai',
		market: 'Medical AI',
		breaks: 'An audit asks what the training changed. No record of it exists, because no architecture in clinical use produces one — so the model goes back.',
		cost: '12–36 months',
		costUnit: 'added to market entry',
	},
	{
		id: 'regulated-finance',
		market: 'Regulated Finance',
		breaks: 'A model that cannot be audited component by component cannot be validated under SR 26-2. A model that is not validated is not deployed.',
		cost: 'The programme',
		costUnit: 'written off in full',
	},
	{
		id: 'data-obligation',
		market: 'Data Obligation',
		breaks: 'A data licence terminates. The clause covers anything trained on it, and retraining from scratch is the only remedy that satisfies the clause.',
		cost: '$100,000–$500,000',
		costUnit: 'per deletion request',
	},
	{
		id: 'defense-safety-critical',
		market: 'Defense & Safety-Critical',
		breaks: 'A system modifies itself in the field and cannot account for it afterwards. That is not a finding a programme remediates.',
		cost: 'The deployment',
		costUnit: 'ended, not remediated',
	},
];

// THE COMPARISON — the section the sale is made in. Third rewrite, and the notes matter because
// the first two failed in opposite directions.
//
// V1 said "No answer exists" five times. A rhetorical device, not an analysis, and an evaluator
// who knows SHAP and Weights & Biases exist discounts the section the moment it pretends
// otherwise.
//
// V2 fixed that and broke something worse. It ran on the ink at --text-small and opacity 0.62,
// which computes to roughly 3.1:1 against #16182B — under the 4.5:1 floor for body text. The most
// important section on the site became the least readable one. It also drifted into the
// vocabulary of the paper: backward pass, saliency methods, probing classifiers, local gradient
// chain, correction tensor.
//
// THE READER IS NOT AN ML ENGINEER. It is the person who has to answer an auditor, sign off a
// model, or explain a deletion request to a client. They know their own domain, not ours. So the
// rule for this copy is: state the concept in their language, give the concrete example, stop.
// Name a tool where naming it is the evidence — SHAP, MLflow — and go no further into mechanism
// than the sentence needs.
const matrix = [
	{
		q: 'What failed, and when?',
		industry: 'A performance chart. It moves only after the damage is done, and it cannot describe anything smaller than the whole run.',
		ormas: 'The component, the step it happened on, and how severe it was.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Is that a finding, or an opinion?',
		industry: 'An estimate, produced afterwards by a separate tool that can itself be wrong.',
		ormas: 'A record, written at the moment of the change, by the system that made it.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Which data caused the damage?',
		industry: 'Current methods cannot tell bad data apart from genuinely difficult data, so they discard both — and the difficult examples were the valuable half.',
		ormas: 'The two are told apart, and neither is discarded.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Can one source be removed later?',
		industry: 'Only by retraining the model from the beginning. Six figures, every time it is asked.',
		ormas: 'What the model built from that source is a named, removable part. Remove it, and issue a certificate of what was removed.',
	},
	{
		q: 'What does accountability cost to run?',
		industry: 'A second model, trained and maintained alongside the first.',
		ormas: 'Nothing. You deploy one model, the same size and speed as the one you would have deployed anyway.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Will an auditor accept it?',
		industry: 'A written account of what the team intended to do.',
		ormas: 'Evidence of what the training actually did, signed and unalterable.',
	},
];

// Named, and credited with what they are genuinely good at. Naming them is the evidence that this
// is a category distinction rather than a boast — which is what 13_COPY_RULES requires.
const alternatives = [
	{
		name: 'SHAP · LIME · Integrated Gradients',
		body: 'Explain why a finished model made one particular prediction. A good answer to a different question — they were never built to watch a model being trained, and do not claim to be.',
	},
	{
		name: 'Weights & Biases · MLflow',
		body: 'Excellent at recording what happened to a training run. They cannot record what happened inside the model.',
	},
	{
		name: 'Co-teaching · DivideMix',
		body: 'Run a second model to get a second opinion, then use it to decide which data to throw away.',
	},
	{
		name: 'Retraining from scratch',
		body: 'The only method that genuinely removes a data source from a model. Six figures, per request, and at scale it is not done at all.',
	},
];


// Tied to published, dated obligations rather than to our own release schedule — the one urgency
// argument on this page that does not depend on us.
const whyNow = [
	{
		framework: 'EU AI Act · Article 12',
		body: 'High-risk AI systems must keep automatic, immutable records of events across the system lifecycle. ORMAS emits that record as a physical property of training rather than as a logging layer bolted alongside it.',
	},
	{
		framework: 'SR 26-2 · SS1/23',
		body: 'The interagency guidance that replaced SR 11-7 in April 2026 keeps the disciplines that matter: model inventory, independent validation, and documented change control. A model that cannot be audited component by component cannot be validated, and a model that is not validated is not deployed.',
	},
	{
		framework: 'GDPR · Article 17',
		body: 'The right to erasure is being enforced against trained models, not only against databases. The compliance industry has already named the category it needs — unlearning-ready architectures — ahead of any supply existing.',
	},
];
---

<Page title="The neural network that can name its own broken part" description={"ORMAS makes per-component causal attribution a physical property of training. The account is produced as the model learns, not reconstructed afterwards."}>
	<!-- 1. HERO. Capability-led: the headline states the thing nothing else can do, and the three
	     figures beneath it are the evidence for that specific sentence, not general credentials. -->
	<section class="hero">
		<div class="hero-inner container">
			<div class="hero-copy">
				<Eyebrow>ORMAS · STRUCTURAL INTELLIGENCE FOR NEURAL NETWORKS</Eyebrow>
				<h1 class="hero-headline">
					A neural network that names its own broken part.
				</h1>
			</div>
			<div class="hero-lede">
				<p class="hero-subhead">
					A training architecture that records what each component of the model did, while it
					learns — rather than a monitoring layer bolted on afterwards.
				</p>
				<div class="hero-cta-row">
					<Button variant="primary" href="/contact">Pre-book ORMAS</Button>
					<Button variant="secondary" href="/technology">Read the paper</Button>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>

		{/* Full width, beneath the copy. It was a 1200x560 strip pinned beside a headline column,
		    which is why it read small no matter what the copy did — at roughly 45% of the
		    container there was never room for the labels. */}
		<div class="hero-art container">
			<HeroVisual />
		</div>

		{/* The trust strip. This was three research figures and a "All figures: CIFAR-10 /
		    CIFAR-100" line — a results table from a paper, in the first viewport of a commercial
		    site. With no customer logos to show, the five markets do the job a logo bar does:
		    they say this is a product with buyers rather than a study with results. The evidence
		    numbers moved down into the evidence section, which is what that section is for. */}
		<div class="hero-trust">
			<div class="hero-trust-inner container">
				<p class="hero-trust-label">Deployed on your own infrastructure, in</p>
				<ul class="hero-trust-list">
					{sectors.map((s) => (
						<li><a href={`/sectors/${s.id}`}>{s.name}</a></li>
					))}
				</ul>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- 2. THE PROBLEM. New section. The page previously went from the headline straight into a
	     mechanism explanation, which asks a visitor to care about an answer before a question has
	     been put to them. Three failures, each with the cost already being paid for it. -->
	<section class="section container reveal">
		<div class="scenario">
			<div class="scenario-col">
				<p class="scenario-label">A run dies at 60 percent. Today —</p>
				<p class="scenario-body">
					The loss curve says the model degraded around step 40,000. It cannot say which component
					caused it, because nothing computed that. So the run restarts from the last good
					checkpoint, blind, and the same compute is paid for twice. On a frontier run that is
					thousands of GPU-hours to arrive back where you already were.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="scenario-col scenario-col--after">
				<p class="scenario-label">The same run, trained under ORMAS —</p>
				<p class="scenario-body">
					The network names the component, the step it failed at, the size of the change, and the
					bound it stayed inside. Not an estimate produced afterwards by a second tool — a
					quantity the network computed about itself while training, which costs nothing to read
					back.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
		<p class="scenario-note">
			The same record answers the auditor asking which data shaped a decision, and the regulator
			asking what changed between two versions. One mechanism, three questions.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="band band--surface section reveal">
		<div class="container">
			<Eyebrow>WHAT IT COSTS YOU TODAY</Eyebrow>
			<h2 class="section-title">Nobody budgets for this. Everyone pays it.</h2>
			<p class="section-lede">
				Every error signal in a neural network touches every parameter at once. That is what makes
				these systems powerful, and it is why <em>which part did this?</em> has no available
				answer — not because the answer is buried, but because there is no part to point at.
				The bill arrives in a different currency in every market.
			</p>

			<div class="ledger">
				<div class="ledger-head">
					<span>Market</span>
					<span>Where it breaks</span>
					<span class="ledger-head-cost">What it costs</span>
				</div>
				{/* The ROW is not a link, deliberately. It was, and two things were wrong with that:
				    a whole-row target with no affordance navigates a reader who did not know they
				    had clicked anything, and it leaks the funnel — this is the second section on
				    the page, and a click here loses the reader before the product, the evidence
				    and the offer. The markets section further down is where routing belongs, and
				    it has real buttons. The market name stays a link, because a word styled as a
				    link is an affordance nobody has to guess at. */}
				{ledger.map((row) => (
					<div class="ledger-row">
						<a class="ledger-market" href={`/sectors/${row.id}`}>{row.market}</a>
						<span class="ledger-breaks">{row.breaks}</span>
						<span class="ledger-cost">
							<span class="ledger-cost-figure tabular">{row.cost}</span>
							<span class="ledger-cost-unit">{row.costUnit}</span>
						</span>
					</div>
				))}
			</div>

			<p class="problem-closer">
				One property is missing from every architecture on that list, and every figure above is a
				consequence of its absence. ORMAS is the correction.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- 3. THE COMPARISON. Back on the light ground: on the ink this ran at roughly 3.1:1 and the
	     most important section on the site was the least readable one. The six capabilities that
	     used to close this section have moved to THE PRODUCT, where they belong — this section is
	     the comparison and nothing else. -->
	<section class="section container reveal proof">
		<Eyebrow>THE COMPARISON</Eyebrow>
		<h2 class="section-title">Everyone else reconstructs what happened. This records it.</h2>
		<p class="section-lede">
			When something goes wrong inside a model, every tool available today works the same way: it
			examines a finished system and produces its best estimate of what must have occurred. The
			estimate is often good. It cannot be verified, and it cannot be filed.
		</p>
		<p class="proof-statement">
			What follows is not a better estimate. It is a different kind of answer — one written while
			the work is happening, by the system doing the work.
		</p>

		<div class="matrix" role="table" aria-label="How the question is answered today, compared with ORMAS">
			<div class="matrix-head" role="row">
				<span role="columnheader">The question you have to answer</span>
				<span role="columnheader">How it is answered today</span>
				<span role="columnheader" class="matrix-head--lit">With ORMAS</span>
			</div>
			{matrix.map((row) => (
				<div class="matrix-row" role="row">
					<p class="matrix-q" role="cell">{row.q}</p>
					<p class="matrix-industry" role="cell">{row.industry}</p>
					<p class="matrix-ormas" role="cell">{row.ormas}</p>
				</div>
			))}
		</div>

		<div class="alts">
			<p class="alts-label">What is being compared</p>
			<div class="alts-grid">
				{alternatives.map((a) => (
					<div class="alts-item">
						<p class="alts-name">{a.name}</p>
						<p class="alts-body">{a.body}</p>
					</div>
				))}
			</div>
		</div>

		<p class="proof-closer">
			None of this is a report produced for you afterwards. It is written by the system as it
			works — which is the whole difference between something you are asked to believe and
			something you can check.
		</p>
	</section>

	<!-- 4. THE PRODUCT — the introduction to what is being sold, and the informational spine of the
	     page. It was a catalogue: a name, a question and three capability tags per feature, which
	     tells a buyer nothing about what they receive or where to begin.
	
	     Two things from the corpus it was missing entirely:
	       · the deliverable is THREE OBJECTS, not a model (`08 §5`) — a buyer who thinks they are
	         buying a model prices it against a model
	       · the ladder is ordered BY HOW HARD IT IS TO SAY YES (`04 §1`), which is the single most
	         useful thing you can tell someone deciding where to start
	
	     The six-cell capability grid is gone. Six abstract verbs at full size told an industry
	     reader nothing; the tags on each row carry the same information where it means something. -->
	<section class="section container reveal">
		<Eyebrow>THE PRODUCT</Eyebrow>
		<h2 class="section-title">ORMAS is a training architecture. What you buy is what it leaves behind.</h2>
		<p class="section-lede">
			Nothing about how you train changes: the same data, the same objective, the same model at
			the end of it. What changes is what exists alongside that model — parts that can be named,
			and therefore protected, repaired, accounted for, separated by source, and removed on
			request.
		</p>

		<div class="deliverables">
			<p class="block-label">What you receive</p>
			<div class="deliverables-grid">
				<div class="deliverable">
					<p class="deliverable-n tabular">01</p>
					<h3>The model</h3>
					<p>
						Deploys exactly as it would have. Same architecture, same inference cost, nothing
						about serving it changes.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="deliverable">
					<p class="deliverable-n tabular">02</p>
					<h3>The named parts</h3>
					<p>
						What the model learned from each source, as a separate object you can point at — bound
						to a record of the data that justified it, and removable.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="deliverable">
					<p class="deliverable-n tabular">03</p>
					<h3>The record</h3>
					<p>
						Every change the training made: which part, when, why, how much, and inside what
						limit. Signed, and diffable against the version you last approved.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="ftable">
			{/* A mono label, not a second headline. This block previously opened with an h3
			    reading "Seven features. One licence." — a second title competing with the section's
			    own, saying less than it, and breaking the symmetry with "What you receive" above.
			    Both blocks are labelled the same way now; the section has one headline. */}
			<p class="block-label">Where it lands hardest</p>
			<p class="ftable-note">
				Those six operations can be combined in many ways. Seven combinations carry almost all of
				the value, because each one lands on a failure a specific market is already paying for —
				the same failures priced at the top of this page. They are set out in the order of what
				each asks of you: the first needs a file and never touches your model; the last needs a
				limit agreed with your reviewer before you begin. <strong>Other combinations exist. These
				are the seven worth naming.</strong>
			</p>

			<div class="ftable-head">
				<span>The use case</span>
				<span>What it does</span>
				<span>What it asks of you</span>
			</div>

			{features.map((f, i) => (
				<div class="ftable-row">
					<div class="ftable-id">
						<p class="ftable-index tabular">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</p>
						<a class="ftable-name" href={`/product/${f.id}`}>{f.name}</a>
						<p class="ftable-q">{f.question}</p>
						<p class="ftable-markets">{marketsFor(f.id)}</p>
					</div>
					<div class="ftable-does">
						<p>{f.summary}</p>
						<ul class="ftable-caps">
							{f.capabilities.map((c) => <li>{c}</li>)}
						</ul>
					</div>
					<p class="ftable-asks">{f.requires}</p>
				</div>
			))}
		</div>

		<p class="product-closer">
			One product, one licence, on your own infrastructure. You turn on what your situation calls
			for and the licence is scoped to that — one integration, one contract, and nothing priced per
			use case.
		</p>

		<Button variant="secondary" href="/product">See the product in full</Button>
	</section>

	<!-- 5. THE EVIDENCE. It was three prose cards that RE-ARGUED the comparison two sections above
	     — "failures get a name and a time" is matrix row 1, "every correction is on the record" is
	     matrix row 6 — followed by a separate strip of three figures. Two blocks, both partly
	     redundant, neither carrying a measured result the comparison had not already claimed.
	
	     It now carries three measured results, each led by its own number, and the separate figure
	     strip is gone into them. The comparison argues; this proves. -->
	<section class="section container reveal">
		<div class="evidence-split">
			<div class="evidence-split-intro">
				<Eyebrow>THE EVIDENCE</Eyebrow>
				<h2 class="section-title">Three results. Every condition stated.</h2>
				<p class="section-lede">
					Each figure comes from a controlled experiment against a parameter-matched standard
					network: same parameter count, same data, same schedule.
				</p>
				<Button variant="secondary" href="/technology">Read the full evidence</Button>
			</div>

			<div class="evidence-rows">
				<div class="evidence-row">
					<p class="evidence-row-figure tabular">80.3%</p>
					<div class="evidence-row-body">
						<h3>Recovery from total structural collapse</h3>
						<p>
							A layer was destroyed mid-training, on a network that had reached 85.1%. The
							standard network stayed at 10% — chance level — on every seed, permanently.
							ORMAS diagnosed the failure within one epoch and climbed back to 80.3%.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="evidence-row">
					<p class="evidence-row-figure tabular">94.6%</p>
					<div class="evidence-row-body">
						<h3>Retained through a shift in the task</h3>
						<p>
							Trained on one task, then another, with no replay buffer and no task identifier —
							the conditions under which a deployed model is normally retrained. The standard
							network kept 47.3% of what it knew.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="evidence-row">
					<p class="evidence-row-figure tabular">22,014</p>
					<div class="evidence-row-body">
						<h3>Stable where standard training failed outright</h3>
						<p>
							A fifty-node network at 30% label noise across 200 epochs. Standard training
							returned NaN and stopped. ORMAS stayed stable, and every one of those corrections
							is on the record with its component, magnitude and limit.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>

		<p class="evidence-qualifier">
			383 controlled experiments across four architecture families. All results on CIFAR-10 and
			CIFAR-100 — <a href="/technology">the paper</a> carries the full conditions, the error bars, and
			the one result that went against us.
		</p>
	</section>

	<!-- 6. MARKETS. Reads from data/sectors.ts and frames each market by the question it asks and
	     the feature that answers it. -->
	<section class="section container reveal">
		<Eyebrow>FIVE MARKETS · ONE PRODUCT</Eyebrow>
		<h2 class="section-title">The mechanism is the same. The vocabulary is yours.</h2>
		<p class="section-lede">
			A hospital calls it the site. A bank calls it the region. A biotech calls it the batch. An
			AI lab calls it the corpus. Every market below asks the same question in its own words,
			and each is answered by one of the seven use cases.
		</p>
		<div class="sector-rows">
			{sectors.map((sector) => {
				const lead = features.find((f) => f.id === sector.primaryFeature);
				return (
					<div class="sector-row">
						<div class="sector-row-tile">
							<SectorGraphic id={sector.id} caption={false} />
						</div>
						<div class="sector-row-body">
							<div class="sector-row-heading">
								<h3>{sector.name}</h3>
								<span class="sector-row-pill">{sector.axis}</span>
							</div>
							<p class="sector-row-question">{sector.question}</p>
							<p class="sector-row-hook">{sector.changes}</p>
							<div class="sector-row-footer">
								<p class="sector-row-stat">
									Answered by <strong>{lead ? lead.name : ''}</strong> · {sector.features.length} of 7 use cases in scope
								</p>
								<Button variant="secondary" size="small" href={`/sectors/${sector.id}`}>Read the market</Button>
							</div>
						</div>
					</div>
				);
			})}
		</div>
		<Button variant="tertiary" href="/sectors">See all five markets</Button>
	</section>

	<!-- 7. WHY NOW. New section. Every other urgency argument on this site is about our own
	     schedule, which is not the buyer's problem. These three are dated, published, and enforced
	     by somebody else. -->
	<section class="section container reveal">
		<Eyebrow>WHY NOW</Eyebrow>
		<h2 class="section-title">The record is becoming an obligation, not a preference.</h2>
		<p class="section-lede">
			Three separate regimes now require of a trained model something standard architectures
			cannot produce: evidence of what changed inside it, attributable to a part. The
			institutions that can generate that record are not merely compliant — they are setting the
			format everyone else will be measured against.
		</p>
		<div class="whynow-grid">
			{whyNow.map((w) => (
				<article class="whynow-item">
					<p class="whynow-framework">{w.framework}</p>
					<p class="whynow-body">{w.body}</p>
				</article>
			))}
		</div>
	</section>

